{ns}
{define: A5 base-fret 1 frets x x 0 0 0 5} 
{define: Cadd9 base-fret 1 frets x 3 2 0 3 0} 
{define: CaddG base-fret 1 frets x 3 2 0 1 3} 
{define: Gadd base-fret 1 frets 3 2 0 0 3 3} 
{t: I KNOW YOU’RE OUT THERE SOMEWHERE – Moody Blues}
{st: (Hayward) }
{artist: Moody Blues  }
{composer: Justin Hayward }


https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cjImFYf2Vzc  (Capo 0) (MC0/1)

TIP:  If transposing, play the root chord of the transposed (Gadd) and (CaddG) adding the 5th note as necessary.

TIP:  About that two-part trill at the end of the chorus, it may look complicated but it's really not.  You start with a (G) and move to an unconventional (A5), where just the 1st string of the 5th fret is emphasized, and back to (G).  There are two ways to play it.  The first way is, from the (G), just extend your pinky to the 1st string of the 5th fret, play the chord, and lift off to get back to (G).  That might be a stretch (literally) for some so I find a simpler way is to just pull off the top two strings of the (G) and slide your bottom finger up two frets the the 5th fret (A5), play the chord, and then slide back down two frets and replace the top strings for the next (G).  Very easy.  For the second part you start with (CaddG) which is nothing more than (C) with your pinky on the 1st string of the 3rd fret.  Lift off the pinky to get back to (C), put your pinky on the 2nd string/3rd fret (same place as before but just up one string) to get (Cadd9), and lift off the pinky to return to (C).  Quite easy.   


